1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,573
On the evening of August 11th, 1986, a British military

2
00:00:04,613 --> 00:00:08,804
helicopter tore across the flat fields of Somerset, in the

3
00:00:08,844 --> 00:00:13,182
south-west of England, at just over 400 kilometers per hour.

4
00:00:13,222 --> 00:00:16,040
About 250 miles per hour.

5
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:18,017
That's not the strange part.

6
00:00:18,057 --> 00:00:20,950
The strange part is what happened next.

7
00:00:20,990 --> 00:00:21,890
Nothing.

8
00:00:21,890 --> 00:00:26,540
Forty years later, no conventional helicopter has officially flown faster.

9
00:00:26,580 --> 00:00:31,420
So what is the fastest military helicopter in the world?

10
00:00:31,460 --> 00:00:34,640
Ask the internet, and you'll get four different answers.

11
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:39,080
A twin-rotor heavy lifter that first flew in 1961.

12
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:41,668
A Soviet gunship nicknamed the flying tank.

13
00:00:41,708 --> 00:00:44,453
That British record-breaker.

14
00:00:44,493 --> 00:00:48,380
And a strange new machine the US Army just picked to replace

15
00:00:48,420 --> 00:00:52,242
the Black Hawk, which might not even count as a helicopter.

16
00:00:52,282 --> 00:00:53,790
They can't all be right.

17
00:00:53,830 --> 00:00:54,872
Or can they?

18
00:00:54,912 --> 00:01:00,357
To find out, we need to understand the invisible wall that every

19
00:01:00,397 --> 00:01:06,100
helicopter slams into, and the sixty-year race to break through it.

20
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:09,940
Let's start with the answer you'll find first.

21
00:01:10,610 --> 00:01:13,453
Answer number one comes from Guinness World Records.

22
00:01:13,493 --> 00:01:18,693
According to Guinness, the fastest military helicopter on the planet

23
00:01:18,733 --> 00:01:22,469
is the Boeing CH-47F Chinook, with a top speed of

24
00:01:22,509 --> 00:01:26,090
315 kilometers per hour, or 196 miles per hour.

25
00:01:26,130 --> 00:01:28,887
At first, that sounds like a joke.

26
00:01:28,927 --> 00:01:30,970
The Chinook is basically a flying school bus.

27
00:01:31,010 --> 00:01:35,246
It can carry around fifty-five troops, or sling an artillery

28
00:01:35,286 --> 00:01:39,307
piece underneath, and its basic design first flew back in

29
00:01:39,347 --> 00:01:39,704
1961.

30
00:01:39,704 --> 00:01:48,107
More than twelve hundred have been built, and over twenty countries still fly them.

31
00:01:48,147 --> 00:01:50,260
So how does a flying bus keep up with attack helicopters?

32
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:52,378
Look at the rotors.

33
00:01:52,418 --> 00:01:56,162
Most helicopters spend a chunk of their engine power

34
00:01:56,202 --> 00:01:59,074
on a small tail rotor, whose only job is

35
00:01:59,114 --> 00:02:02,567
to stop the body from spinning the opposite way.

36
00:02:02,607 --> 00:02:04,782
The Chinook doesn't need one.

37
00:02:04,822 --> 00:02:09,029
Its two big rotors turn in opposite directions and cancel each other

38
00:02:09,069 --> 00:02:12,214
out, so all of that power goes into lift and speed.

39
00:02:12,254 --> 00:02:13,572
But there's a catch.

40
00:02:13,612 --> 00:02:16,250
Not everyone measures top speed the same way.

41
00:02:16,290 --> 00:02:21,313
Some manufacturers quote true airspeed, some quote indicated airspeed, and some

42
00:02:21,353 --> 00:02:25,607
quote the never-exceed speed, a limit the pilot should never cross.

43
00:02:25,647 --> 00:02:33,392
On paper, a few helicopters beat the Chinook, depending on whose spreadsheet you read.

44
00:02:33,432 --> 00:02:35,730
Which brings us to answer number two.

45
00:02:36,550 --> 00:02:41,650
In the 1970s, the Soviet Union built a helicopter unlike anything in the West.

46
00:02:41,690 --> 00:02:45,996
The Mil Mi-24, known to NATO as the Hind, was a heavily

47
00:02:46,036 --> 00:02:51,133
armored gunship that could also carry eight soldiers in the back.

48
00:02:51,173 --> 00:02:53,939
Its crews nicknamed it the crocodile.

49
00:02:53,979 --> 00:02:56,420
Others called it a flying tank.

50
00:02:56,460 --> 00:02:59,100
Tanks are not supposed to be fast.

51
00:02:59,140 --> 00:03:02,021
But the Hind had a sleek body, retractable

52
00:03:02,061 --> 00:03:05,290
landing gear, and two powerful turbine engines.

53
00:03:05,330 --> 00:03:11,061
And on September 21st, 1978, the Soviets set out to prove a point.

54
00:03:11,101 --> 00:03:15,088
They took a modified Hind, removed its stub wings to

55
00:03:15,128 --> 00:03:18,727
cut drag, gave it the cover name A-10, and flew

56
00:03:18,767 --> 00:03:22,212
it over a measured course near Moscow at 368.

57
00:03:22,252 --> 00:03:24,329
4 kilometers per hour.

58
00:03:24,369 --> 00:03:26,933
That's 229 miles per hour, and it was

59
00:03:26,973 --> 00:03:30,170
the new absolute speed record for helicopters.

60
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:34,025
Its modern descendant, the Mi-35M, is still listed

61
00:03:34,065 --> 00:03:37,880
among the fastest combat helicopters flying today.

62
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:41,404
And for eight years, the Soviets owned the title.

63
00:03:41,444 --> 00:03:43,760
Then the British decided to take it back.

64
00:03:44,470 --> 00:03:47,355
The Westland Lynx was a small, nimble helicopter

65
00:03:47,395 --> 00:03:49,731
flown by the British Army and the Royal

66
00:03:49,771 --> 00:03:50,076
Navy.

67
00:03:50,076 --> 00:03:54,328
In 1986, Westland took its own company demonstrator,

68
00:03:54,368 --> 00:03:58,867
registration G-Lynx, and turned it into a rotor-powered

69
00:03:58,907 --> 00:03:59,650
dragster.

70
00:04:00,350 --> 00:04:04,675
The engineers fitted uprated Rolls-Royce Gem 60 engines, cleared to

71
00:04:04,715 --> 00:04:08,779
run at their emergency rating for about forty-five percent more

72
00:04:08,819 --> 00:04:09,210
power.

73
00:04:09,210 --> 00:04:13,988
They added water-methanol injection for an extra boost, reshaped the

74
00:04:14,028 --> 00:04:18,026
exhausts to push out some bonus thrust, and borrowed tail

75
00:04:18,066 --> 00:04:22,277
surfaces from another Westland helicopter to keep it stable.

76
00:04:22,317 --> 00:04:24,314
But the real secret was on top.

77
00:04:24,354 --> 00:04:28,867
G-Lynx carried experimental rotor blades called BERP blades, with strange

78
00:04:28,907 --> 00:04:32,236
paddle-shaped tips, swept back like the wing of a jet.

79
00:04:32,276 --> 00:04:37,036
Westland had developed them together with the UK Ministry

80
00:04:37,076 --> 00:04:41,500
of Defence, to make rotors faster and more efficient.

81
00:04:42,210 --> 00:04:45,853
On the evening of August 11th, test pilot Trevor Egginton

82
00:04:45,893 --> 00:04:49,084
and flight test engineer Derek Clews lined up on a

83
00:04:49,124 --> 00:04:54,059
fifteen-kilometer course over the Somerset Levels, near Glastonbury, and flew

84
00:04:54,099 --> 00:04:57,871
it twice, in opposite directions, so the wind couldn't help

85
00:04:57,911 --> 00:04:58,234
them.

86
00:04:58,234 --> 00:04:59,574
Their average was 400.

87
00:04:59,614 --> 00:05:00,948
87 kilometers per hour.

88
00:05:00,988 --> 00:05:04,485
That's 249 miles per hour.

89
00:05:04,525 --> 00:05:07,665
On one of the runs, they touched almost 413.

90
00:05:07,705 --> 00:05:11,612
It smashed the Hind's record, and it still stands today.

91
00:05:11,652 --> 00:05:15,910
The FAI, the body that certifies aviation records, still

92
00:05:15,950 --> 00:05:20,056
lists it as the absolute speed record for helicopters.

93
00:05:20,096 --> 00:05:26,130
So officially, the fastest helicopter flight ever recorded was made by a military Lynx.

94
00:05:26,170 --> 00:05:30,140
And there's one detail that explains why nobody has beaten it.

95
00:05:30,180 --> 00:05:33,180
At 400 kilometers per hour, the tips of

96
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:36,219
its rotor blades were moving at Mach 0.

97
00:05:36,259 --> 00:05:37,159
97.

98
00:05:37,187 --> 00:05:40,190
That's ninety-seven percent of the speed of sound.

99
00:05:40,780 --> 00:05:44,510
To see why that matters, picture a helicopter rotor from above.

100
00:05:44,550 --> 00:05:48,837
In a hover, every blade moves through the air at the same speed.

101
00:05:48,877 --> 00:05:52,258
On a helicopter like the Lynx, the blade tips are

102
00:05:52,298 --> 00:05:56,587
already doing almost 800 kilometers per hour, just spinning in

103
00:05:56,627 --> 00:05:57,046
place.

104
00:05:57,046 --> 00:05:58,219
Now fly forward.

105
00:05:58,259 --> 00:06:01,485
On one side, the advancing blade swings into the

106
00:06:01,525 --> 00:06:04,683
wind, so the helicopter's speed is added to it.

107
00:06:04,723 --> 00:06:08,005
On the other side, the retreating blade swings away from

108
00:06:08,045 --> 00:06:10,972
the wind, so the helicopter's speed is taken away.

109
00:06:11,012 --> 00:06:15,059
At 400 kilometers per hour, the advancing tip is doing

110
00:06:15,099 --> 00:06:19,070
nearly 1,200, right at the edge of the sound barrier.

111
00:06:19,110 --> 00:06:23,010
The retreating tip is crawling along at under 400.

112
00:06:23,050 --> 00:06:26,537
And close to the hub, something even stranger happens.

113
00:06:26,577 --> 00:06:30,331
The air actually flows over the retreating blade backwards.

114
00:06:30,371 --> 00:06:34,890
On G-Lynx, that reverse flow covered roughly half of the blade.

115
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:37,389
That creates two problems at once.

116
00:06:37,429 --> 00:06:41,157
On the advancing side, shock waves start to form on

117
00:06:41,197 --> 00:06:45,221
the blade tips, and drag, vibration and noise shoot up.

118
00:06:45,261 --> 00:06:48,241
On the retreating side, the slow blade has to tilt

119
00:06:48,281 --> 00:06:50,839
to a steeper angle to make the same lift as

120
00:06:50,879 --> 00:06:54,161
the fast one, or the helicopter would simply roll over.

121
00:06:54,201 --> 00:06:58,054
Tilt it too far, and the airflow tears away from the blade.

122
00:06:58,094 --> 00:07:00,390
That's called retreating blade stall.

123
00:07:00,430 --> 00:07:05,720
The nose pitches up, the helicopter rolls, and the pilot can lose control.

124
00:07:06,310 --> 00:07:08,270
So why not spin the rotor faster?

125
00:07:08,310 --> 00:07:11,921
Then the advancing tip hits the sound barrier even sooner.

126
00:07:11,961 --> 00:07:13,014
Slow it down?

127
00:07:13,054 --> 00:07:15,370
Then the retreating blade stalls sooner.

128
00:07:15,410 --> 00:07:17,610
Engineers are trapped between the two.

129
00:07:17,650 --> 00:07:23,458
That's why almost every helicopter in service tops out around 300 kilometers

130
00:07:23,498 --> 00:07:28,383
per hour, and why G-Lynx needed exotic blades just to touch 400.

131
00:07:28,423 --> 00:07:29,766
It's not an engine problem.

132
00:07:29,806 --> 00:07:31,950
It's a wall.

133
00:07:32,780 --> 00:07:34,853
So engineers asked a simple question.

134
00:07:34,893 --> 00:07:38,104
What if the rotor didn't have to do everything?

135
00:07:38,144 --> 00:07:40,975
A normal helicopter's rotor has three jobs at once:

136
00:07:41,015 --> 00:07:45,429
it lifts the aircraft, pulls it forward, and steers it.

137
00:07:45,469 --> 00:07:48,490
A compound helicopter hands some of that work to other parts.

138
00:07:48,530 --> 00:07:51,714
Wings take over some of the lift at high speed,

139
00:07:51,754 --> 00:07:55,144
and a propeller does the pushing, so the rotor can

140
00:07:55,184 --> 00:07:55,595
relax.

141
00:07:55,595 --> 00:07:57,988
The US Army tried this back in 1967, with

142
00:07:58,028 --> 00:08:01,607
the Lockheed AH-56 Cheyenne, a futuristic gunship with stubby

143
00:08:01,647 --> 00:08:04,040
wings and a pusher propeller in its tail.

144
00:08:04,080 --> 00:08:08,677
It reached about 215 knots in level flight, close

145
00:08:08,717 --> 00:08:13,029
to 400 kilometers per hour, in the late 1960s.

146
00:08:13,069 --> 00:08:17,276
But it was expensive, complicated and late, and

147
00:08:17,316 --> 00:08:20,529
in August 1972 the Army canceled it.

148
00:08:20,569 --> 00:08:21,734
Remember that name.

149
00:08:21,774 --> 00:08:23,000
It's coming back.

150
00:08:23,590 --> 00:08:27,221
Decades later, Sikorsky came at the problem from a different angle.

151
00:08:27,261 --> 00:08:31,084
Its X2 demonstrator had two rigid rotors stacked

152
00:08:31,124 --> 00:08:34,465
on top of each other, spinning in opposite

153
00:08:34,505 --> 00:08:35,390
directions.

154
00:08:35,390 --> 00:08:39,625
That means there's always an advancing blade on both sides of the

155
00:08:39,665 --> 00:08:43,835
aircraft, so the retreating blades don't have to carry the load.

156
00:08:43,875 --> 00:08:48,427
At high speed, the X2 slowed its rotors to keep the tips

157
00:08:48,467 --> 00:08:53,511
below the sound barrier, while a propeller in the tail did the

158
00:08:53,551 --> 00:08:54,207
pushing.

159
00:08:54,207 --> 00:09:00,798
On September 15th, 2010, it reached 250 knots in level flight.

160
00:09:00,838 --> 00:09:04,768
That's about 463 kilometers per hour, much faster

161
00:09:04,808 --> 00:09:08,171
than G-Lynx, but the record was never made

162
00:09:08,211 --> 00:09:08,940
official.

163
00:09:08,940 --> 00:09:11,385
The team still won the Collier Trophy, one

164
00:09:11,425 --> 00:09:13,930
of the biggest prizes in American aviation.

165
00:09:13,970 --> 00:09:21,381
Three years later, Eurocopter's X3, with short wings and two propellers, pushed

166
00:09:21,421 --> 00:09:27,605
that unofficial mark to 255 knots, around 472 kilometers per hour.

167
00:09:27,645 --> 00:09:31,460
Sikorsky turned the X2 idea into military prototypes.

168
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:33,711
First the S-97 Raider, a light scout.

169
00:09:33,751 --> 00:09:37,097
Then, together with Boeing, the much bigger SB-1

170
00:09:37,137 --> 00:09:39,990
Defiant, built to replace the Black Hawk.

171
00:09:40,030 --> 00:09:44,516
In October 2021, Defiant hit 247 knots, about 457

172
00:09:44,556 --> 00:09:49,134
kilometers per hour, in level flight, and its test

173
00:09:49,174 --> 00:09:53,290
pilot said there was still engine power left.

174
00:09:53,330 --> 00:09:59,875
In Europe, Airbus's Racer demonstrator has since reached 444 kilometers per hour.

175
00:09:59,915 --> 00:10:01,880
The wall had been broken.

176
00:10:01,920 --> 00:10:05,330
So why isn't the Army flying any of these today?

177
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,816
Because a completely different idea beat them all.

178
00:10:08,856 --> 00:10:14,200
Instead of helping the rotor, why not turn it into a propeller?

179
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:16,318
That's a tiltrotor.

180
00:10:16,358 --> 00:10:20,372
It takes off like a helicopter, then tilts its rotors

181
00:10:20,412 --> 00:10:24,884
forward and flies like an airplane, with wings carrying the

182
00:10:24,924 --> 00:10:25,459
weight.

183
00:10:25,459 --> 00:10:27,920
The US Marines have flown the V-22 Osprey this way for years.

184
00:10:27,960 --> 00:10:31,676
And in December 2017, Bell flew a new and

185
00:10:31,716 --> 00:10:36,439
much faster tiltrotor for the Army, the V-280 Valor.

186
00:10:36,479 --> 00:10:38,970
In testing, it reached 305 knots.

187
00:10:39,010 --> 00:10:43,589
That's about 565 kilometers per hour, or 350 miles

188
00:10:43,629 --> 00:10:48,393
per hour, faster than any helicopter has ever flown.

189
00:10:48,433 --> 00:10:51,612
The production version is designed to carry fourteen

190
00:10:51,652 --> 00:10:54,151
soldiers at more than 300 miles per hour.

191
00:10:54,191 --> 00:10:59,661
In December 2022, the Army chose the V-280 over Sikorsky and Boeing's

192
00:10:59,701 --> 00:11:05,571
Defiant, in what was described as its biggest helicopter decision in forty

193
00:11:05,611 --> 00:11:06,090
years.

194
00:11:06,090 --> 00:11:11,870
Then, in February 2024, it canceled its future scout helicopter program too,

195
00:11:11,910 --> 00:11:16,925
the one the Raider was built for, saying drones had changed aerial

196
00:11:16,965 --> 00:11:18,073
reconnaissance.

197
00:11:18,113 --> 00:11:22,540
The fastest compound helicopters ever built were left without a buyer.

198
00:11:22,580 --> 00:11:28,088
And in April 2026, the Army gave its new aircraft an official name:

199
00:11:28,128 --> 00:11:30,728
the MV-75 Cheyenne II.

200
00:11:30,768 --> 00:11:34,547
It honors the Cheyenne tribes, and it's a nod to

201
00:11:34,587 --> 00:11:39,084
that canceled AH-56 Cheyenne, the Army's first big bet on

202
00:11:39,124 --> 00:11:39,601
speed.

203
00:11:39,601 --> 00:11:44,350
More than half a century later, the name Cheyenne gets a second chance.

204
00:11:45,020 --> 00:11:48,528
So, what is the world's fastest military helicopter?

205
00:11:48,568 --> 00:11:51,575
It depends on what you call a helicopter.

206
00:11:51,615 --> 00:11:57,254
If you want the official title for a military helicopter in service,

207
00:11:57,294 --> 00:12:02,765
Guinness says it's the CH-47F Chinook, at 315 kilometers per hour.

208
00:12:02,805 --> 00:12:07,057
If you want the fastest helicopter flight ever certified, it's G-Lynx, at 400.

209
00:12:07,097 --> 00:12:09,730
87, a record that's now forty years old.

210
00:12:09,770 --> 00:12:13,529
If you count experimental compound helicopters, the X2

211
00:12:13,569 --> 00:12:17,186
and Defiant went faster still, but never officially.

212
00:12:17,226 --> 00:12:20,769
And if you just want the fastest machine the Army

213
00:12:20,809 --> 00:12:24,718
will use to carry soldiers into battle, it's the MV-75

214
00:12:24,758 --> 00:12:28,520
Cheyenne II, which isn't really a helicopter at all.

215
00:12:28,560 --> 00:12:30,874
Every one of them is fighting the same enemy:

216
00:12:30,914 --> 00:12:34,261
a spinning blade that can't go faster than sound on

217
00:12:34,301 --> 00:12:37,781
one side, and can't slow down without stalling on the

218
00:12:37,821 --> 00:12:38,220
other.

219
00:12:38,220 --> 00:12:42,790
As for the record holder, G-Lynx is still around.

220
00:12:42,830 --> 00:12:46,760
It sits in a helicopter museum in Weston-super-Mare, not far

221
00:12:46,800 --> 00:12:50,465
from the course where it made history, still waiting for

222
00:12:50,505 --> 00:12:54,170
a conventional helicopter fast enough to take its crown.

223
00:12:54,210 --> 00:12:57,888
If you enjoyed this, subscribe for more stories

224
00:12:57,928 --> 00:13:01,052
about the machines that push the limits.

225
00:13:01,092 --> 00:13:02,687
And tell us in the comments:

226
00:13:02,727 --> 00:13:05,140
which one do you think deserves the title?

